1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
A NASA sensor flying over the Mojave
Desert spotted something nobody

2
00:00:03,727 --> 00:00:04,609
had recorded there.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:08,243
Topaz. And it found it
without touching the ground.

4
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:10,100
Quick note before we go on.

5
00:00:10,308 --> 00:00:11,899
You might have noticed a new voice.

6
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:15,765
From now on, Orbital Atlas is narrated
by the person who makes it.

7
00:00:15,939 --> 00:00:20,448
Me. This is the ER-2, one
of the highest-flying planes NASA has.

8
00:00:20,622 --> 00:00:23,002
It cruises at about sixty-five
thousand feet,

9
00:00:23,025 --> 00:00:25,254
almost twice as high as a passenger jet.

10
00:00:25,289 --> 00:00:29,016
Up there, the pilot wears a pressure
suit a lot like an astronaut's.

11
00:00:29,109 --> 00:00:31,419
Below, the desert looks barren and brown.

12
00:00:31,501 --> 00:00:35,018
But to the sensors on this plane,
it shines in hundreds of colors.

13
00:00:35,065 --> 00:00:37,027
The key sensor is called AVIRIS.

14
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:40,045
The newest version is about
the size of a microwave oven,

15
00:00:40,069 --> 00:00:42,066
and it has to stay cold to work.

16
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,572
It splits reflected sunlight
into hundreds of wavelengths,

17
00:00:45,595 --> 00:00:47,917
from visible light into the infrared.

18
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:51,864
Every mineral reflects light
in its own pattern, like a fingerprint.

19
00:00:51,922 --> 00:00:56,021
Match the fingerprint, and you know what
the ground is made of, pixel by pixel.

20
00:00:56,532 --> 00:00:59,481
NASA first flew AVIRIS in 1986.

21
00:00:59,504 --> 00:01:02,952
Instruments like it have since
studied every major rocky world

22
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:04,542
from Mercury to Pluto.

23
00:01:04,658 --> 00:01:07,654
Now this technology is pointed
back at the United States.

24
00:01:08,095 --> 00:01:11,891
The project is called GEMx,
run by NASA and the U.S.

25
00:01:11,938 --> 00:01:13,284
Geological Survey.

26
00:01:13,668 --> 00:01:17,731
Its target is critical minerals,
the raw materials behind phones,

27
00:01:17,789 --> 00:01:20,157
wind turbines and electric car batteries.

28
00:01:20,227 --> 00:01:21,841
In 2023, the U.S.

29
00:01:21,864 --> 00:01:24,290
imported its entire supply
of twelve of them.

30
00:01:24,674 --> 00:01:29,329
Since 2023, GEMx flights have covered
more than three hundred eighty-six thousand

31
00:01:29,376 --> 00:01:32,801
square miles of American soil,
including most of California.

32
00:01:32,835 --> 00:01:37,688
In 2025 alone, the ER-2 flew thirty-six
science missions and collected

33
00:01:37,735 --> 00:01:39,453
more than seven billion measurements.

34
00:01:39,569 --> 00:01:44,120
Then the data pointed somewhere specific:
a rocky ridge near Barstow, California.

35
00:01:44,155 --> 00:01:46,581
The sensor had picked up
the signature of topaz,

36
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:48,648
where nobody had identified it before.

37
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:51,481
So geologists hiked
in to check, with rock picks,

38
00:01:51,539 --> 00:01:53,698
hand lenses and a heavy bag of samples.

39
00:01:53,721 --> 00:01:54,941
Testing confirmed it.

40
00:01:54,975 --> 00:01:57,205
Topaz. They were not hunting for gems.

41
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:00,908
Topaz forms under the same volcanic
conditions as porphyry copper,

42
00:02:00,955 --> 00:02:02,975
one of the world's main sources of copper.

43
00:02:02,998 --> 00:02:07,143
And copper is the third most used metal
on Earth, after steel and aluminum.

44
00:02:07,178 --> 00:02:08,873
Is there copper under this ridge?

45
00:02:09,024 --> 00:02:09,999
Nobody knows yet.

46
00:02:10,034 --> 00:02:13,226
That will take ground-penetrating
equipment and a lot more work.

47
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:14,480
It is not the only clue.

48
00:02:14,654 --> 00:02:18,776
Near Barstow, the survey also found
hectorite, a lithium-bearing clay,

49
00:02:18,799 --> 00:02:20,819
in the waste rock of an abandoned mine.

50
00:02:21,028 --> 00:02:23,722
What was once thrown away
could become a resource.

51
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:29,713
This spring, the ER-2 flew out of Colorado
to scan Colorado, Utah, New Mexico,

52
00:02:29,748 --> 00:02:31,373
Arizona and Texas.

53
00:02:31,547 --> 00:02:34,751
Twenty-six flights, more than one
hundred twenty-five hours.

54
00:02:34,752 --> 00:02:37,771
People have prospected
the American West for generations.

55
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,836
Seen from the edge of space,
there is still a lot left to find.

56
00:02:40,987 --> 00:02:45,294
For another place that looks completely
different from above, watch this one next.
